    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent high-level overview, but it feels a bit thin on the actual legal mechanics. Could you go deeper into the Originalist position? I'm interested in how they specifically handle the \"well regulated Militia\" clause versus the right of the individual, and what kind of historical evidence they typically rely on to make their case.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists fundamentally argue that the prefatory \"well regulated Militia\" clause is merely a statement of purpose, not a limitation on the operative clause guaranteeing the right to keep and bear arms. They rely heavily on colonial-era documents and early state constitutions to demonstrate that the right was understood as an individual defense right, not solely a collective military necessity. This historical evidence, they contend, proves the Founders intended a robust, individual right protected against government infringement.",
      "source": "target",
